NXR-181|Independent RISC-V feature matrix|RISC-V configuration|Qualification shall build baseline, Zaamo-only, M/U-PMP-only and combined profiles as four independent configurations.|Atomics and privilege protection are separate axes and should not become one coupled feature.|Each profile reports its actual ISA and privilege capability; disabling either extension preserves baseline behavior.|Compile and execute profile-specific positive and illegal-operation fixtures.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Single hart; fixed four-region PMP profile where enabled; no implicit MMU.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|Atomics and privilege protection are separate axes and should not become one coupled feature.
NXR-182|Zaamo capability mismatch|RISC-V configuration|The platform shall reject an atomic-capable build when the selected execution target or RAM window lacks the required Zaamo contract.|Instruction availability alone does not establish atomic behavior for every mapped address.|Wrong-capability manifests fail before workload release; baseline fallback uses its explicitly selected implementation.|Run manifest mismatch controls and RAM versus MMIO atomic address fixtures.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|No atomics on device memory; no silent runtime emulation inside the hardware qualification profile.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|Instruction availability alone does not establish atomic behavior for every mapped address.
NXR-183|Atomic return-value oracle|RISC-V configuration|Zaamo qualification shall check both stored values and returned old values for every supported operation.|Final memory checks can miss an incorrect register result from an atomic instruction.|Exchange, add, OR and AND return and store the expected words; signed min and max are tested only when separately advertised by the instruction model.|Execute edge operands and randomized word pairs; mutate return-value handling to prove detection.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Fixed operand corpus plus declared seed and operation count; no heap on target.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|Final memory checks can miss an incorrect register result from an atomic instruction.
NXR-184|Atomic ordering witnesses|RISC-V configuration|Atomic memory ordering claims shall be limited to the declared single-hart interrupt and device-ordering model.|A single-hart stress pass cannot establish multicore memory-order semantics.|Release-acquire publication under preemption succeeds; unsupported multicore ordering is reported unsupported.|Run interrupt handoff litmus tests and inspect emitted fences with the selected compiler.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|No SMP qualification claim; bounded shared ordinary-RAM words.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|A single-hart stress pass cannot establish multicore memory-order semantics.
NXR-185|User entry register sanitation|RISC-V configuration|An optional U-mode entry shall initialize privilege state and all exposed register contents according to a documented ABI.|Processor U-mode support does not by itself supply a safe operating-system user boundary.|Entry cannot inherit privileged stack pointers or stale kernel data; legal user entry reaches its declared PC.|Use register-pattern fixtures and inspect mstatus, mepc and return privilege on an independent execution model.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One declared user frame per slot; no FP or vector state unless separately enabled.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|Processor U-mode support does not by itself supply a safe operating-system user boundary.
NXR-186|PMP boundary access matrix|RISC-V configuration|Four-region PMP qualification shall test the last allowed and first forbidden byte for every configured access permission.|Region setup needs boundary evidence rather than only checking CSR readback.|Read, write and execute violations trap with the expected cause and address; permitted boundary accesses succeed.|Generate byte, halfword and word probes including region-crossing accesses on RTL and interpreter.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Exactly four declared regions; reject unrepresentable layouts before enabling user execution.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|Region setup needs boundary evidence rather than only checking CSR readback.
NXR-187|PMP lock semantics|RISC-V configuration|PMP lock qualification shall verify the documented reset-only unlock behavior and machine-mode access effects.|A simulated region table can miss architectural lock effects and their reset lifetime.|Writes to locked configuration fail to change it; accesses obey the selected locked permissions until reset.|Attempt configuration and address rewrites and compare reset and pre-reset behavior.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|No runtime unlock API; fixed board reset domain.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|A simulated region table can miss architectural lock effects and their reset lifetime.
NXR-188|Trap origin integrity|RISC-V configuration|Trap dispatch shall preserve and report the originating privilege mode without allowing user-controlled state to impersonate kernel origin.|Shared trap entry must distinguish a legitimate user request from a kernel exception.|User ecall and illegal CSR access are classified correctly; nested fault fixtures retain original context evidence.|Compare saved status and cause against independently calculated trap transitions.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Fixed trap frames; bounded nesting policy explicitly declared.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|Shared trap entry must distinguish a legitimate user request from a kernel exception.
NXR-189|Interpreter timer service modes|RISC-V configuration|The interpreter shall separate instruction-exact timer delivery from opt-in batched delivery and report the configured delay bound.|Reducing timer checks can improve throughput while changing interrupt placement and must be visible.|Batch size one agrees with exact delivery; batch size ten never exceeds its declared instruction delay in eligible execution.|Use known-deadline instruction streams, branch loops and masked-interrupt cases with reference traces.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Batch parameter finite and recorded; no timing-accuracy label for relaxed delivery.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|Reducing timer checks can improve throughput while changing interrupt placement and must be visible.
NXR-190|Interpreter RTL differential trace|RISC-V configuration|Instruction-model qualification shall compare architectural retire state with RTL for matched finite workloads.|Fast interpretation needs an independent oracle rather than self-comparison of final signatures.|Register, PC, trap cause and touched memory first diverge at a recorded instruction identity; an injected opcode defect is detected.|Run short deterministic instruction, atomic and privilege corpora with identical reset state.|NXR-102,NXR-139|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Bounded trace window and operation count; model speed does not imply cycle accuracy.|include/rtos_hal.hpp,include/rtos_hal_atomic.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.hpp,sim/interpreter/machine.cpp,ports/riscue/context.S|Fast interpretation needs an independent oracle rather than self-comparison of final signatures.
